# The `in` of a for clause is a delimited reserved word too: `inx` is the word
# `inx`, not `in` glued to `x`. The delimited `In` token does not match `in` as a
# prefix, so `for i inx a; do y; done` is rejected.
<<<cmd
printf 'for i inx a; do y; done\n' | "$TP" run std.shell --input -
<<<assert
exit-not 0
